  "findings": [
    {
      "anchor": "plan.html:916",
      "id": "done_when-1",
      "reason": "the worker cannot satisfy the count with the stated fixture, so it must either silently move all 2,000 rows into the queried project and drop the multi-project claim, or weaken the assertion, and a SPEC-exact worker is blocked instead.",
      "text": "the fixture holds 2,000 run rows across 3 projects, but the asserted calls are four calls of `stored_run_rows` \"with the same arguments\", and `stored_run_rows` takes `project` as a required positional filter that `_record_rows` applies in SQL (reckon/run_store.py:907, 491-520), so a query for one project decodes only that project's share (about 667 of the 2,000); \"decode the 2,000 rows once in total\", and the negative control's \"four times\", are both unsatisfiable as written",
      "type": "done_when",
      "would_change": true
    },
    {
      "anchor": "plan.html:916",
      "id": "done_when-2",
      "reason": "the deliverable promises \"the answers are the same as at the base revision\" for every caller, and without a second argument set against the unchanged store the node's gate cannot fail on the wrong-cache-key defect it is most likely to ship.",
      "text": "every assertion uses one argument set, so a cache keyed on the change token alone, ignoring `project`, `run_ids`, `fields`, `plan` and `node`, passes the whole measure and its negative control while returning one reader's rows to another; the section's own Why lists readers whose filters differ (`run_ids=[run_id]` in review.py, promotion_release.py and pending_landing.py, `fields=(\"run_id\",)` in ledger.py:2871, `node=node_id` in preparation.py:143)",
      "type": "done_when",
      "would_change": true
    }
  ],
